findings.description:
The doc says "Concurrency with the operator's own worktrees, disk use, and cleanup on failure are the slice-level questions". Yet the doc also declares "Failure modes are enumerated and observable" as a principle, and the list there leaves out every worktree failure:
- add fails;
- a stale worktree is left by a crashed run (it stays registered in `git worktree list`);
- removal fails.

A crash between `git worktree add` and removal leaves a registered worktree in the operator's repo. That repo is the "never mutate" surface. It also clashes with this project's own worktree conventions (`cf worktree list`). At minimum the architecture should fix two things:
- the worktree's location: outside the repo, in squadron's data dir;
- the naming that keeps worktrees reclaimable on the next run.

Review-a-PR and post-findings share the adapter, the PR record and persistence. `sq pr create` brings different machinery:
- a new one-shot model composition path;
- slice detection via `cf`;
- integration-branch targeting;
- a "body section contract other tooling can rely on";
- a traceability rule borrowed from initiative 360.

Under the motivation's own framing (enterprise repos, PR-centric review), it is not needed. Its "stable section structure that `sq review pr` can parse" also creates a coupling back into the review path that nothing else requires.

Defer `sq pr create` and the body contract to a follow-on initiative once the adapter has proven out. Keep only the protocol's `create_pr` operation if it is cheap. Also drop "parse for intent and claimed verification" until a consumer exists.
